    "created_at": "2016-01-20T00:02:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-01-20T00:03:09Z",
    "body": "> Well they can be, if the href attribute gets set.\n\nI guess so; I was under the impression that that clause was only meant for blob URLs per the note (\"This is only observable for blob: URLs as parsing them involves the structured clone algorithm.\").\n\n> The given value to hash would have nothing to parse against.\n\nI don't quite understand. Let's say it's a URL with its non-relative flag unset, or with null url. Then step 1 of href's setter calls reinitialise url, which with my changes does not terminate, and calls set the url. That sets the element's url to the result of parsing its href against the document base URL. Good! The url is now updated against any recent changes to href and to the document base URL. Now we proceed as normal, updating url with the given value.\n\nIn contrast, if the element has its a non-relative flag set, then it won't get reinitialised to take into account any base URL or href changes, as desired.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
